  "section_notes": "The final rules adopt strong S1 reporting (24h/72h, all incidents, retroactive disclosure) but weaken S2 by setting a permanent 7% failure limit above the 5% safe threshold while keeping per-model results non-public. S3 gives new units a 24-month shut-off deadline (weakened vs ≤12 months acceptable) and a 72-month retrofit with a feasibility alternative; S4 requires built-in suppression at 36 months with possible equivalent approval; S5 keeps an 8/100k mandatory recall trigger but allows a time-limited regulator-approved corrective alternative, and notices are only 'promptly'. Overall the rules favor Northwind, especially on S2, S3, and S5."
